Never wl_global_destroy a wl_output global right after wl_global_remove.

libwayland's registry_bind posts the fatal "global wl_output (N) is unavailable" error for a
bind to a global that is no longer in the display's list, i.e. one already destroyed. A global
that was only removed can still be bound, which is why a removed global should be destroyed some
time later, once clients have seen the removal (wlroots waits 5 s, wlr_global_destroy_safe).

Hyprland destroyed it at once: retireOutputGlobal() (a monitor with the same name added again)
dropped the last reference to a removed output with no bound resources, and
CWLOutputProtocol::destroyResource() dropped a removed output as soon as its last resource was
released. On a quick monitor flicker (an HDMI connector that comes up, goes away and comes back
within a second or two on resume) every client has a bind in flight for the announced output,
so Xwayland, the shell, portals, input methods and browsers were all disconnected at once.

remove() now records when the global was removed and arms a timer. canDestroy() is true only
5 s after the removal with no resource bound; retireOutputGlobal() keeps a retired output in
defunctOutputs until then, destroyResource() destroys only then, and the timer's sweep drops
whatever has become destroyable from defunctOutputs and outputs. Binds in that window get an
inert resource (output-late-bind-inert.patch). isGlobalPrivileged() also looks in
defunctOutputs, so a sandboxed client's bind to a retired output isn't filtered into the same
fatal error.
diff --git a/src/managers/ProtocolManager.cpp b/src/managers/ProtocolManager.cpp
index bf20269..fcaa5e9 100644
--- a/src/managers/ProtocolManager.cpp
+++ b/src/managers/ProtocolManager.cpp
@@ -103,7 +103,8 @@ static void retireOutputGlobal(const std::string& name) {
     if (!old->isDefunct())
         old->remove();
 
-    if (old->hasBoundResources())
+    // keep it (and its global) until clients have seen the removal and released it
+    if (!old->canDestroy())
         PROTO::defunctOutputs.emplace_back(old);
 }
 
@@ -354,6 +355,12 @@ bool CProtocolManager::isGlobalPrivileged(const wl_global* global) {
             return false;
     }
 
+    // a removed output global is still bindable until it is destroyed
+    for (auto const& v : PROTO::defunctOutputs) {
+        if (global == v->getGlobal())
+            return false;
+    }
+
     // this is a static whitelist of allowed protocols,
     // outputs are dynamic so we checked them above
     // clang-format off
